# etude-aul.4 plan

ANCESTOR: etude-aul (P3) — empirically compare phase-gate prompt variants against recorded artifacts and ground-truth labels; the measured prototype result was Variant B at 2/2 versus Variant A at 1/2, and the three closed siblings now provide the corpus, evaluator, and scoring machinery this CLI exposes.

BLAST RADIUS: none: worktree and repo only. Gate scratch and built-binary manual-test fixtures use one bounded temporary directory each and are removed. Every Go verification command, including focused tests, `make test`, `make lint`, `make build`, and `go install`, sets `GOCACHE` and `GOMODCACHE` beneath that throwaway root; `GOBIN` is also scoped there, so verification neither overwrites the host's installed `etude` nor warms shared Go caches. No host packages, services, containers, credentials, or shared state are changed.

TRUST BOUNDARY: prompt variant files, labels JSON, cohort selector, and recorded run artifacts are user-controlled inputs and therefore untrusted. Existing parsers, identifier validation, refstore reads, and ExecJudge isolation are the enforcement points. The configured judge command is operator-trusted executable code; arbitrary same-process code execution is outside scope.

TOPOLOGY: single foreground CLI process. It evaluates cells sequentially; there are no new goroutines, shared writers, or concurrent mutation paths.

DURABILITY CLASS: at-most-once per process invocation for each variant/fixture cell. Gate-bench evaluation is read-only with respect to run refs and does not persist eval results; a judge may have its own external effects under the existing trusted judge-command contract.

COMPLETENESS ENUMERATION: the complete user-visible surface is (1) the unchanged replay-vs-original mode, (2) gate-mode argument and flag validation, (3) built-in run-refs cohort resolution, (4) canonical prompt-file identity and one evaluation for every variant/fixture cell, (5) optional explicit labels keyed exactly by run_id+stage+round with zero-match rejection, progression-proxy fallback, source counts, and loud circularity warnings, (6) comparable per-variant confusion-matrix rates computed only over the common successfully evaluated fixture set, (7) per-cell verdict rendering including unlabeled and failed cells, and (8) clear non-zero exit when no complete cross-variant fixture remains. This is a single-site CLI orchestration change; no other command registration or storage writer is changed.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: none

## Decision: extend the existing command

Extend the current `etude bench` Cobra leaf with a mutually exclusive gate-benchmark mode rather than add `etude bench gate`.

The decision is grounded in `internal/cli/bench.go` as landed:

- `bench` is one leaf command with the shipped positional form `bench <stage>`, not an existing command group.
- It already owns judge resolution, judge-model isolation, timeouts, the `--last` cohort limit, dependency injection for tests, and human-readable aggregate/per-cell reporting.
- The required public syntax is `bench --gate <phase> ...`. Converting the leaf into a command group would either break `bench <stage>` or require a default-subcommand compatibility shim, adding ambiguity and regression risk without adding useful separation.
- Gate mode does fundamentally different work (evaluate recorded artifacts directly rather than replay them), so the implementation will make the modes explicit and mutually exclusive: conditional argument validation, gate-only/replay-only flag rejection, and separate `runReplayBench`/`runGateBench` orchestration paths behind the same command.

`--cohort run-refs` selects the built-in `bench.RunRefsSource`; `--last N` remains the shared size selector. The injected `bench.CorpusSource` seam remains available to tests and future adapters. Unknown cohort selectors fail clearly; this bead does not invent or import a beads/Dolt adapter.

Each `--variant` value is a prompt file path and its exact flag value is the stable display name in output. Before checking distinctness, paths are made absolute, cleaned, and symlinks resolved, so `a.md`, `./a.md`, and a symlink to `a.md` cannot masquerade as different variants. Failure to resolve a symlink is a clear validation error; there is no cleaned-path fallback that could silently treat aliases as distinct. Gate mode requires at least two canonically distinct variants, `--cohort`, and `--judge` (or existing judge config).

`--labels` is optional and uses the already-landed strict JSON schema. A label key is exactly the tuple `run_id`, `stage`, and positive integer `round`, matching `Fixture.Provenance.RunID`, `.Stage`, and `.Round`. Explicit labels take precedence for matching tuples; missing explicit labels fall back to the progression proxy. When a labels file is supplied but matches zero selected fixtures, the command fails before invoking the judge rather than silently presenting proxy-only scores as explicitly labelled. Mixed explicit/proxy cohorts are allowed, but each variant report prints explicit, proxy, and unlabeled counts beside its denominator and prints the existing circularity warning whenever any proxy label contributes.

Judge failures remain per-cell evidence, but aggregate variant scores use only complete fixtures for which every variant returned a verdict. All successful and failed cells are still rendered, and each excluded fixture is named with the variant or variants whose cell failed so a shrinking denominator is explainable. If no fixture succeeds for all variants, the command exits non-zero. Variants render in flag order and cells in corpus-source fixture order, with variant as the inner order; no map iteration controls output. This makes every variant's rate use the same fixture keys and denominator; a 1/1 variant is never compared against a 5/5 variant after asymmetric failures.

## Implementation

Change only the allowed files:

- `internal/cli/bench.go`
  - add gate-mode flags and conditional Cobra argument validation while preserving `bench <stage>` behavior;
  - add an injectable `bench.CorpusSource` field and production resolver for `run-refs`;
  - canonicalize and validate distinct prompt files, failing on symlink-resolution errors, and validate optional strict labels JSON, rejecting a supplied labels file that matches zero selected fixtures;
  - evaluate every variant/fixture cell through `eval.GateEvaluator` using exactly one `gate-prompt` context input;
  - convert `Score.Passed` through `bench.GateVerdictFromPassed`, resolve labels, retain only complete cross-variant fixtures for comparable aggregation with `bench.ScoreGatePredictions`, and render common denominators, explicit/proxy/unlabelled counts, every successful/failed cell, warnings, and failures;
  - continue after an individual cell failure, name excluded fixtures and failing variants, but exit non-zero if no fixture succeeds for all variants; preserve variant flag order and corpus fixture order in output.
- `internal/cli/bench_test.go`
  - extend the existing injected-command harness with a fake corpus source and content-aware gate judge;
  - preserve all existing replay-mode tests unchanged in meaning.
- `docs/bench.md`
  - document both modes, exact syntax, selector/variant/labels contracts, proxy warning and final-plan limitation, output orientation, partial failures, and manual examples.

## Planned regressions

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateHappyPathReportsVariantScoresAndCells` — two variants over labeled fixtures produce distinct confusion matrices and every cell row.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateUsesProgressionProxyAndWarns` — absent labels use the proxy and print the exact circularity warning.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateExplicitLabelsOverrideProxy` — explicit labels win for a matching key.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateLabelsFileWithZeroMatchesFails` — a syntactically valid labels file whose run_id+stage+round tuples match no selected fixture fails before judge invocation.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateReportsExplicitProxyAndUnlabeledCounts` — a mixed cohort exposes the provenance composition of every rate.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGatePartialJudgeFailureUsesCommonFixtureSet` — one asymmetric failed cell is reported, and every variant score excludes that fixture and uses the same remaining denominator.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateNoCompleteFixtureExitsNonZero` — cell successes that leave no fixture complete across all variants return a clear error instead of incomparable rates.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateRejectsAliasesOfOneVariantPath` — path spelling and symlink aliases do not satisfy the two-distinct-variant requirement.

Planned regression: `TestBenchGateValidation` — rejects positional args in gate mode, fewer than two/distinct variants, missing or unknown cohort, unreadable variant/labels files, invalid labels, and replay-only flags; every table row asserts its specific error so a neighbouring failure cannot false-pass it.

Planned regression: `TestBenchReplayModeRejectsGateOnlyFlags` — legacy mode fails clearly instead of silently ignoring gate flags.

Planned regression: `TestBenchHelpDocumentsGateMode` — help shows the required `--gate`, repeated `--variant`, `--cohort`, and optional `--labels` surface while retaining legacy flags.

Planned regression: existing `TestBenchHappyPath` and the complete current bench test suite remain green, proving `bench <stage>` compatibility.

